WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.259
NICHOLS: This is part of the Emerging Technology
Series at the Council on Foreign Relations,

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:12.170
and today's topic is "Biotechnology: Potential
and Perils of Innovation."

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.900
My name is Rod Nichols, and I'm honored to
be presiding today.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.560
You're in for a treat.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.180
Those of you wanted an intellectual treat,
you're in for a treat because it's a brilliant

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.400
panel.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:32.210
They're both deep and broad, which is not
often the case in these kinds of topics.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:39.470
And the topic is hot in science and engineering
circles in our country and around the world,

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:44.120
for national policies and priorities around
the world.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.990
One of the technologies that will be discussed
by some of my more knowledgeable colleagues,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:56.350
CRISPR, was named "Breakthrough of the Year"
last year by a major scientific society.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:01.410
So my job is, first, the plan for the meeting
is I will introduce the speakers very briefly.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.180
You have their bios.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.050
They'll then make a few remarks-you'll hear
what they're going to be talking about in

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.760
a moment-and then we'll have your comments
and questions.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:19.390
Drew Endy, on the screen looking very thoughtful
and waving a finger or two at us-(laughter)-is

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.920
professor of genetic engineering at Stanford,
developed a new lab.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:32.350
He's really a pioneer in this field himself,
a leader globally, an innovator for sure,

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.450
and an advisor on policy.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:39.740
Laurie Garrett, on my immediate left, has
been an incisive leader at the Council on

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:44.090
Foreign Relations for many years, as many
of you know, in global health.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.930
She is fearless, as you can see from her crutches.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.420
She will go anywhere, ride her bike up and
down anywhere, from Croatia to Manhattan.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.980
She got hurt recently, but it doesn't seem
to have affected her head at all.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:56.980
(Laughter.)

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:02.330
She is deeply informed and globally alert,
which is one of the things we want to have

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:03.680
emphasis at the Council.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.009
Julie Gerberding, on my far left, has had
a remarkable career, as you can see in her

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:11.009
bio.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:15.230
I asked her a few minutes ago whether she
would object to my calling her a renaissance

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:20.260
woman, whether that was politically incorrect,
and she said, well, she thought that was OK.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:25.060
Very few people have done as many different
kinds of things as she has done.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:31.620
Her work spans medicine at the bedside, with
research at the bench, and at the highest

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.910
level of policy circles, including her current
work at Merck.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:42.870
What we're going to start with, I'm asking
each one of the panelists to give us one example

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:49.420
of the potential for contemporary biotechnology,
which is very powerful, and one example, as

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.540
they see it, of a peril.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.990
And we're going to start with Drew from Stanford.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.920
So, Drew, you're up.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.770
ENDY: Rod, thanks so much.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.390
Can you hear me OK?

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.390
NICHOLS: Yes.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:02.390
ENDY: Great.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:09.110
You know, I thought I would offer an example
from the realm of medicine, and talk about

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:14.560
opiates, medicines that are used to treat
pain-morphine, hydrocodone, and so on.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.580
NICHOLS: Good.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:21.950
ENDY: Right now, the sourcing of these medicines
is realized by growing poppies-agricultural

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:24.890
fields of poppies, opium poppies, that are
harvested.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:28.849
And the compounds are extracted and brought
into preparations and used to treat pain.

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:33.440
There are a number of problems with this current
practice.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.540
As most are aware domestically, we have massive
problems with addiction.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.760
We have side effects from the medicines themselves.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.060
They're declared as essential medicines by
the World Health Organization.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.890
And that's important because when you look
at who has access to these medicines, only

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:55.720
about 2 1/2 billion people on Earth have access
to these essential medicines.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.470
So what does this have to do with the frontiers
of biology and biotechnology?

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:04.030
The reason I'm sorry to be not able to join
you in person, my wife is expecting with our

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.440
second child due shortly so I'm not allowed
to travel.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:13.209
But last year, she and her technical team
were able to recapitulate the biosynthesis

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:20.339
of opiates in baker's yeast, so that you could
realize the same biochemistry and the same

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:25.629
final active pharmaceutical ingredients, not
by starting with a field of poppies, but by

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:30.599
brewing-starting with sugar and a fermenter
and an engineered strain of yeast to realize

00:04:30.599 --> 00:04:33.629
the production of the same active pharmaceutical
ingredients.

00:04:33.629 --> 00:04:38.419
Now, the technical significance of this, set
aside what the compounds are, is they are

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:45.069
performing about 30 different biochemical
steps, 30 different enzymes brought from different

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:50.439
organisms into baker's yeast to make an engineered
strain that's able to do a significant amount

00:04:50.439 --> 00:04:52.430
of biochemistry.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.289
Why might they wish to do this?

00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:58.210
Well, if you could make a diversity of these
molecules, maybe you'd eventually have a chance

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:02.869
of screening for alternates which don't have
some of the side effects related to addiction

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:06.490
or respiratory depression or so on-i.e., we
could just get better medicines.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:09.330
That would be a promise of some ambition.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:15.029
Moreover, because the material supply chain
is based on brewing and not poppy farming-for

00:05:15.029 --> 00:05:20.029
example, we might source much of these materials
from poppies grown in Tasmania today in the

00:05:20.029 --> 00:05:24.370
United States, if we will-if we have a brewing
process that could be distributed, maybe the

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:30.069
chance of changing the material supply chain
and providing treatments for paying globally

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:32.309
in a much more responsible way.

00:05:32.309 --> 00:05:33.309
So that's a promise.

00:05:33.309 --> 00:05:37.419
Now, typically when I bring up this example
or others bring up this example the perils

00:05:37.419 --> 00:05:39.449
are straightforward.

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:44.069
There's an illicit market around these compounds
of some significance.

00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:45.860
There's many existing problems.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:50.819
And na�ve deployment of a new supply chain
could lead to more addiction, and so on and

00:05:50.819 --> 00:05:53.000
so forth.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.469
Let me zoom out then and try and put this
in some context.

00:05:56.469 --> 00:06:00.580
At Stanford, we're posing within the engineering
school a question.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.759
The question is: How good can we get at engineering
living matter?

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:10.139
Much like 50 years ago we posed questions
like how good could we get at programing computers.

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:15.560
And we're 10 or 15 years into answering that
question, not just at Stanford but globally.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.719
And the early indications are, we're going
to get really good at engineering living matter.

00:06:20.719 --> 00:06:26.169
As good as you might imagine as we got with
engineering silicon semiconductor based systems.

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:33.240
So then the peril, in my mind, is to figure
out how we can create and sustain venues where

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:39.810
a strategy is realized, a strategy that is
needed to determine what it would mean to

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:44.800
flourish on top of future biotechnologies,
but also a strategy to mitigate some of the

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:45.800
negative outcomes.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:51.120
And I'm sure we'll talk about safety, so I
don't want to preempt that, but I bring up

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:57.331
security as a particular topic not because
I view it as a massive imminent threat, but

00:06:57.331 --> 00:06:59.020
a threat that's developing over time.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:03.289
And to speak practically in the United States
and elsewhere, but certainly within the United

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:08.809
States, we have no venue-politically, from
a policy perspective, or even a technical

00:07:08.809 --> 00:07:15.849
perspective-we have no venue where scholarship
can be sustained and a strategy for realizing

00:07:15.849 --> 00:07:18.999
long-term effective biosecurity might be realized.

00:07:18.999 --> 00:07:23.689
So I think that's the biggest peril we're
facing, just simply an absence of sustained

00:07:23.689 --> 00:07:30.419
dialogue around what strategies might be for
realizing a full-on flourishing bioeconomy,

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:34.370
combined with venues by which we can mitigate
some of the minuses.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:35.789
NICHOLS: Great.

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:37.749
Thank you very much, Drew.

00:07:37.749 --> 00:07:42.599
Laurie will give you her idea of a potential
and a peril.

00:07:42.599 --> 00:07:48.599
GARRETT: Well, yesterday the Recombinant DNA
Advisory Committee, the RAC, at NIH, which

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:55.150
was founded back in the 1970s, as the result
of a prior revolution in biology, the original

00:07:55.150 --> 00:08:01.430
genetic engineering revolution, approved the
first experiment on human beings with CRISPR,

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:03.520
which will be conducted shortly.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:07.330
And it is funded by Sean Parker, billionaire
of Napster fame.

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:10.839
So you see a confluence of different interests
here.

00:08:10.839 --> 00:08:19.000
And the target is cancer cells and modifications
that could result in curing people of cancer.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:26.789
So we're on our first big step towards the
utilization of this technology in human beings.

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:32.469
And certainly we will see some extraordinary
breakthroughs that will be very, very exciting

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:33.750
in the near future.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:41.370
I would say the thing I'm most enthused about
from my own, you know, bias as potential applications

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:48.530
of synthetic biology, CRISPR, and where it
overlaps with basic microbiome research-the

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:56.000
other great revolution going on at the same
time-is in developing a far broader understanding

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:04.110
of pathogens, whether they're viruses, bacteria,
parasites, all over the world, understanding

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:10.580
the ecological niches they occupy, and having
a better appreciation for how they can rapidly

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:17.070
diagnose them, how we can assess their likelihood
of jumping zootomically from one animal species

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:25.000
to our species or to our livestock, or from
one wild plant to an essential food grain

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.710
of some sort.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:33.240
So I'm very, very excited about where this
technology could go in that direction.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:40.570
Of course, like all new biologies, we see
that things get dictated heavily by where

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:43.420
the stock market chooses to invest.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:48.080
And I don't know if the stock market is interested
in preventing pathogens.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:54.450
There may be other priorities that Sean Parker
and his pals devote their money to.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.180
And that takes to my downside concern.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:08.960
We're only-our understanding of how the microbial
world works is so primitive, it's phenomenal

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:12.280
how little we know.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:17.510
We have only relatively recently come to appreciate
that every human being in this room has far

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.870
more microbes in you than cells with your
own DNA in them.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:26.330
We're only at the cusp of appreciating how
those microbes interact with other things

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:33.160
around you-around your chair, on that table,
in the food you just ate-much less how the

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:39.540
same interactions are occurring with animals
and plants and so on, in the oceans, in the

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.660
air we breathe, in the soil, et cetera.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:51.650
I am very concerned that we will have untoward
consequences, mistakes made, that result in

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:58.550
altering some essential microbial balance
that has been in place for millennia, and

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:03.280
that is very vital to some ecological system
that we care about.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.760
It might be the human body.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:06.750
It might be the body of a dog.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.430
It might be soil that we grow wheat it.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:10.430
I don't know.

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:12.040
It could be many things.

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:16.770
And the reason I'm concerned about this is
that-and by the way, this mean then that we're

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:21.060
deliberately or unintentionally altering evolution,
right?

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:27.460
I mean, a fundamental change can occur, especially
using gene drive, that results in an absolute

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.560
switch in the evolutionary trajectory.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:36.530
And I worry about this because the technology,
especially CRISPR, is so darned easy.

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:39.680
You can now buy kits and do it at home.

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.490
There's a whole DIY movement developing.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:45.010
It's cheap.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.370
High school students are learning how to do
these things.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:52.250
Drew has played a vital role in enhancing
the capacities in high schools.

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:54.100
And I think that's fantastic.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:12:02.400
But I'm worried about what escapes our attention
in our hubris that we think we know the world.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.790
And something we do could make a fundamental
change.

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:11.200
NICHOLS: In a few minutes we might come back
and give our group a tutorial on what is CRISPR,

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:16.380
but it's basically gene editing-take out a
gene or put in a gene.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.510
Julie, what are your potential and peril favorites?

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:24.190
GERBERDING: Well, first of all, thank you
so much for letting me participate in this

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:25.190
panel.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:28.940
We actually had a preface to this meeting
yesterday on the phone, and we probably could

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:33.250
have talked all day because we were exploring
these ideas in a great deal of granularity.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:38.550
But I think I'll come back to Drew's approach
and really talk about medicine because, yes,

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:45.470
we will be starting the first clinical trials
using CRISPR gene editing to modify our body's

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:52.370
own immune cells, the T-cells, in such a way
that they are either more aggressive in attacking

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:56.820
cancers, or the breaks that have been put
on them are lost.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:02.040
And obviously this is of tremendous interest
on a global basis, and something that could

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:07.200
extend what we're currently learning from
the new immuno-oncology drugs that have been

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.670
in the news-the Jimmy Carter drug, for example.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:16.610
But it's just taking that same concept and
accelerating it, and allowing us to think

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:23.940
about truly novel ways of attacking cancers,
using our own natural immune system, which

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:29.960
is certainly something that should be more
preferable than chemotherapy or radiation.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:31.420
So it's very exciting.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:34.770
As Laurie said, these technologies are cheap.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:42.370
We'll see an extraordinarily rapid uptake,
I'm sure, and a pressure to do more and more.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:44.680
And the peril is obvious.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:45.680
It's safety.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:47.900
It's the specificity of these techniques.

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:54.400
Are they only editing the DNA that we want
them to edit, or are they actually spilling

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.740
over and affecting other parts of the body?

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:03.680
When you're doing this technique outside of
the body, that threat is not so important.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:09.920
But when you're giving this in ways that can
be inherited-for example, affecting the germ

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:17.120
cells so that the children of treated people
are affected, or you can spread ecologically

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:23.990
across species or into the biome or into the
environment in unexpected ways.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:33.060
We have to very much cautious our enthusiasm
for the benefits with a very humble recognition

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:37.290
that there is an awful lot we don't know right
now about how these techniques are being spread.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:43.650
And to extend that a bit more globally, right
now, for example, in China, the amount of

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:50.950
effort that's going into applying this gene
editing and gene drive technology is extraordinary.

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:56.550
If you do a Google search for gene editing
and China and look at what comes up, you'll

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:04.610
see the myriad applications in rice, in silk
worms, in human embryos, in various other

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.100
cell lines and tissues.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:11.570
So on a global basis we're seeing people run
with this technology in environments that

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:16.630
don't necessarily have a RAC or any of the
other mechanisms that we try to use in the

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:20.400
United States to balance technology with a
safety assessment.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:25.400
And I think that creates a threat, even in
well-intentioned people, let alone the people

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:29.510
who can adapt this technology for more nefarious
purposes.

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:33.660
And CRISPR did appear-well, it wasn't named
CRISPR-but gene editing did appear on the

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:38.570
list of weapons of mass destruction of national
interest just very recently.

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:40.660
So there is a downside.

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:46.240
NICHOLS: The theme in all three of your remarks
have been the sort of political, cultural,

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:51.480
ethical, regulatory environment in which this
powerful new technology is being introduced.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:54.680
And we probably ought to come back to that.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:59.990
Drew, you didn't make a particular recommendation,
but how would you proceed to maximize the

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.470
potential and minimize the peril?

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:05.900
And we'll ask the other panelists to comment.

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:09.580
We certainly don't want to suppress the science
and engineering.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:17.890
ENDY: Yeah, I think the most important thing
I could try to relate is how much of a vacuum

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:25.070
there is around sustained, strategic thinking
and scholarship around where we're trying

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:26.460
to get to.

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:32.360
So if we were talking about nuclear security
for a moment, I bet most everybody in the

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:38.940
room could summon up schools of thought and
strategy-you know, mutual assured destruction

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:45.300
and other approaches to mitigating risks associated
with nuclear threats.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:51.500
How many people in the room could start to
rattle off the names of strategies for mitigating

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.730
biological threats?

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:59.630
And speaking as somebody who is a practitioner,
both of the biotechnology but also in the

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:03.290
circles of biosecurity to a degree, there's
just a gap there.

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:08.370
It's not that there aren't good ideas, but
those ideas aren't being turned over and debated

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:13.709
and worked through with the vigor and rigor
that I would wish to see, let alone being

00:17:13.709 --> 00:17:18.549
coupled to strategic investment in public
health and science and technology development.

00:17:18.549 --> 00:17:23.569
Again, it's not that such things aren't happening,
but they're happening on a very idiosyncratic

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:25.910
and ad hoc basis.

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:30.590
And to the extent that biology is central
to many of the things we care about-starting

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:36.679
with ourselves, but certainly the environment-and
to the extent that our civilization is going

00:17:36.679 --> 00:17:41.799
through a potential transition where, for
example, when I was born there were 3 1/2

00:17:41.799 --> 00:17:45.730
billion people on Earth, and now there's twice
as many people.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:50.619
When I was born, the ecologists tell me there
were twice as many animals in the natural

00:17:50.619 --> 00:17:53.929
environments as there are today, at least
the ones we can see.

00:17:53.929 --> 00:17:59.879
You know, how do we transition our civilization
from living on Earth to living with Earth.

00:17:59.879 --> 00:18:04.880
And what is the role of advanced biology and
biotechnology in navigating and realizing

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:06.309
that transition?

00:18:06.309 --> 00:18:11.149
We need to have a very holistic set of conversations
and sustained scholarship around what our

00:18:11.149 --> 00:18:12.590
strategy should be.

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:15.940
So I'll just-I'll just park it there.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:18.539
There's a lot more to say, but, yeah, I think
that's the biggest gap.

00:18:18.539 --> 00:18:20.190
NICHOLS: Laurie, I think you have-wanted to
make-thank you.

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:24.580
GARRETT: Well, I mean, Drew's put his finger
on the number-one issue.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:29.340
And many things that are-so just to back up
a second, because there may be some that are

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:33.929
a little bit bewildered, and you're not quite
up to speed on some of the terms we've used.

00:18:33.929 --> 00:18:39.580
So let me just say that what we're looking
at now with CRISPR-and that's an acronym,

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:47.950
and it doesn't matter what it stands for-but
it turns out it's a very ancient mechanism,

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:56.639
billions of years old, that microbes have
used, particularly bacteria, to expel unwanted

00:18:56.639 --> 00:19:05.299
entry of genetic material brought in by phage
or viruses that invaded the bacteria.

00:19:05.299 --> 00:19:11.809
So you can think of it as a roughly 3-billion-year
old immune system.

00:19:11.809 --> 00:19:16.370
It's only very recently that our species,
homo sapiens, discovered this ancient thing

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:21.070
that's been out there forever and has been
the way that bacteria maintain their genetic

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:29.090
integrity against the constant onslaught of
phage in their environments, and probably

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:35.200
the way they plucked useful things from the
phage when they needed them, such as the capacity

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.690
to override antibiotics.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:45.690
Now, the problem is we discovered this, it
turns out to be so easy, wow, boom, cheap.

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:51.419
And then discovered there's another layer
to it, which is gene drive, which allows you

00:19:51.419 --> 00:19:59.200
to essentially tell a genetic segment to just
keep on running, and to run in the next generation

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:04.110
and the next generation and the next generation,
so that it becomes a permanent feature on

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:05.509
the landscape.

00:20:05.509 --> 00:20:15.909
Now, just last week I was web in on a briefing
at the NIH, where the heads of two institutes

00:20:15.909 --> 00:20:18.700
were briefing the head of the NIH about Zika.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:21.159
It was a terrifying meeting.

00:20:21.159 --> 00:20:26.919
But at one point Francis Collins asked Tony
Fauci, the head of the National Institute

00:20:26.919 --> 00:20:31.940
of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, well,
what about genetically modified mosquitos?

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:35.299
Can't we use CRISPR to take care of this problem?

00:20:35.299 --> 00:20:41.120
And then we won't have mosquitos carrying
dengue, chikungunya, yellow fever, and Zika.

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:45.399
And Tony, very wisely said, well, there's
two problems with that idea.

00:20:45.399 --> 00:20:48.779
Even though the stock market, by the way,
really likes it and is spending a lot of money

00:20:48.779 --> 00:20:50.139
in this.

00:20:50.139 --> 00:20:54.700
One, these mosquitos only travel in their
lifetime about 200 yards.

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:59.549
So if you're going to release a single generation
CRISPR, it's going to have to be released

00:20:59.549 --> 00:21:05.580
every 200 yards, which is a pretty tough way
to control mosquitos.

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:09.500
And then, if you're going to say, well, no,
we want it to go on forever and make a gene

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:13.720
drive technology, you better know what you're
doing and you better have a really good idea

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:19.350
how you're effecting evolution out there if
you deliberately and permanently alter Aedes

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.669
aegypti mosquitos.

00:21:21.669 --> 00:21:24.570
And Tony's advice was let's not do it.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.039
So that gets to what Drew said.

00:21:27.039 --> 00:21:34.450
Where are the-where is that wisdom over time,
that builds a series of baselines about the

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.269
yes and the no?

00:21:36.269 --> 00:21:37.269
Where are the lines?

00:21:37.269 --> 00:21:38.369
How do we decide?

00:21:38.369 --> 00:21:39.369
What's the process?

00:21:39.369 --> 00:21:41.360
Where do we go?

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:46.750
Many elements of what we're in right now as
a debate, a brand-new debate in its infancy,

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:54.669
overlaps with-starting with the Asilomar 1975
meeting when scientists themselves said, whoa,

00:21:54.669 --> 00:21:58.429
genetic engineering, if we alter viruses,
what might happen?

00:21:58.429 --> 00:22:01.429
Let's all come together and make a rule book.

00:22:01.429 --> 00:22:05.499
That went on for some time, and some permutations.

00:22:05.499 --> 00:22:14.070
Then in early 2000s, we had Dual Use Research
of Concern, DURC, arose, because of deliberate

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:20.649
modifications of flu viruses that were going
on to make super-virulent viruses and to determine

00:22:20.649 --> 00:22:24.480
how likely they were to infect human cells.

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:30.299
This raised a mountain of concerns among bioterrorism
and national security experts, and brought

00:22:30.299 --> 00:22:33.299
all these issues to the fore again.

00:22:33.299 --> 00:22:40.389
Then, this similar pattern arose just a few
years later, with so-called Gain-of-Function

00:22:40.389 --> 00:22:46.649
research, or GOF, the idea that you would
deliberately insert a genetic capacity into

00:22:46.649 --> 00:22:53.529
a microorganism in order to see if that made
it more virulent or that made it more transmissible

00:22:53.529 --> 00:22:55.950
across a broader range of species.

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.919
Once again, all the red flags went up.

00:22:58.919 --> 00:22:59.919
Everybody freaked out.

00:22:59.919 --> 00:23:02.320
The same debate over and over again.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:06.400
And now here we are in the next stage of the
revolution.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:08.570
And I think Drew's absolutely right.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:10.480
We don't have a learning curve.

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:18.090
We have a very tiny cluster of people in most
of the key high-tech countries in the world

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:20.940
who, when we have meetings, we see familiar
faces.

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.880
Oh, right, I saw you ten years ago at the
DURC meeting.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:26.070
You know, here we are again.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:33.080
And yet, we don't seem as-Drew's metaphor
is perfect-we don't see as we did with nuclear,

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:40.350
both fusion and fusion research, a permanent
raising of the bar of the discussion so that

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:46.860
each layer is a learning curve over the prior,
and the sort of assumptions among nations

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:47.860
improve.

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:53.679
And I would just add that-as a final note-that,
you know, everything that we think are the

00:23:53.679 --> 00:23:58.399
values and the norms that we would aspire
to in the United States on these issues are

00:23:58.399 --> 00:24:00.690
quite different in other countries.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:06.129
And let's-we don't have to talk about Russia,
China, you know, Iran, Pakistan, what have

00:24:06.129 --> 00:24:07.129
you.

00:24:07.129 --> 00:24:08.350
Let's just look at the U.K.

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:15.899
Their Human Subjects Committee decided it
was fine to make alterations in gametes with

00:24:15.899 --> 00:24:20.700
the intention of improving in vitro fertilization.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:26.150
So something that we think is kind of taboo
here, and we haven't even had an effort to

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:30.509
consider it formally, is already OK in the
U.K.

00:24:30.509 --> 00:24:33.009
We'll see if Brexit is-
NICHOLS: Julie, since Laurie's last remark

00:24:33.009 --> 00:24:38.100
is about medicine, medicine has an old view,
do no harm.

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:44.570
Are you worried about the ethical implications
of the gene editing in terms of the progress

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:45.570
of medicine?

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:46.879
GERBERDING: Well, absolutely.

00:24:46.879 --> 00:24:49.909
And particularly in the germ line example
that Laurie just gave.

00:24:49.909 --> 00:24:54.730
I think we have to be extremely cautious in
that front.

00:24:54.730 --> 00:25:00.759
And I totally agree that we need a system
of risk assessment and of the rulebook, so

00:25:00.759 --> 00:25:05.509
to speak, that applies globally, although
I also believe that's going to be a very hard

00:25:05.509 --> 00:25:07.510
energy to harness.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:11.690
But it's also true that we've been here and
done this before, not just going back to the

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:13.059
gain-of-function opportunity.

00:25:13.059 --> 00:25:18.470
I was part of that conversation at CDC, since
we were part of the science behind all of

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:19.470
that.

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:26.129
But you know, go back to even, you know, smallpox
vaccine or, you know, the early days of vaccinology,

00:25:26.129 --> 00:25:31.799
where the same debates occurred between the
technology of advancing the opportunity to

00:25:31.799 --> 00:25:35.779
prevent infectious disease versus the safety
concerns and the academe.

00:25:35.779 --> 00:25:44.190
So these are ongoing tensions that exist between
scientific advances and concerns for unknown

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:45.190
risks.

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:50.350
I can't really think of an example where the
science has stopped because of the safety

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:55.480
concerns, but I do think that the boundary
conditions are important to face up to.

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:58.070
And in this case, the stakes are particularly
high.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:03.909
NICHOLS: Do you think we've made any mistakes
so far, with respect to the potential medical

00:26:03.909 --> 00:26:04.909
applications?

00:26:04.909 --> 00:26:10.100
GERBERDING: Well, what we might not understand
and the mistakes that we've actually made.

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:15.210
And, you know, the biome is one example where
I don't think we really fully understand what

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:19.609
our manipulation, even our antimicrobial use,
for example, or anything that put we into

00:26:19.609 --> 00:26:24.129
our bodies, what's that really doing to our
internal ecosystems, let alone the environmental

00:26:24.129 --> 00:26:26.369
ecosystem.

00:26:26.369 --> 00:26:31.299
And if you take an ecological approach and
really think about emerging infectious disease

00:26:31.299 --> 00:26:39.200
and their zoonotic origins, about 80 percent
of the time we really probably have underestimate-have

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:42.369
underestimated the perturbations that we've
caused in the system.

00:26:42.369 --> 00:26:48.149
So, you know, I don't have a startling example
to give you, but I have a strong sense that

00:26:48.149 --> 00:26:50.039
we probably don't know what we don't know.

00:26:50.039 --> 00:26:52.700
GARRETT: That's a good way of putting it.

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:54.190
Can I throw a question to Drew?

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:55.419
NICHOLS: Sure, please.

00:26:55.419 --> 00:27:00.129
GARRETT: To Drew, I point, you know, in the
air, wherever.

00:27:00.129 --> 00:27:01.460
(Laughs.)

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:09.070
Your colleague, George Church, coined a phrase,
directed evolution, about three or four years

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:17.200
ago, partly because of what Craig Venter was
doing out in San Diego with deliberately creating

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:23.169
previously non-existent microorganisms from
nucleotides on up.

00:27:23.169 --> 00:27:31.750
And George was talking, OK, directed evolution,
the idea that humans are not just in the Anthropocene

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:34.700
affecting evolution because we're throwing
pollutants out and what have you, but we're

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:37.989
making actual, deliberate choices.

00:27:37.989 --> 00:27:43.889
And he also convened a meeting recently at
Harvard that was, quote, "secret" to talk

00:27:43.889 --> 00:27:48.529
about directed production of a human genome.

00:27:48.529 --> 00:27:54.989
Can you talk about both the concept of directed
evolution, and what was this secret meeting

00:27:54.989 --> 00:27:58.770
about, and how do you feel about the idea
of it being secret?

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:00.390
NICHOLS: Hmm.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:05.070
ENDY: I'm happy to respond to some of those
questions.

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:07.259
(Laughter.)

00:28:07.259 --> 00:28:13.330
So there's directed evolution, as Pim Stemmer
and Frances Arnold and others have practiced

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:19.600
for a long time, which is in a laboratory
to create diversity and then select to provide

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:25.799
the features you want, sort of like breeding
of a dog, but with molecules and cells.

00:28:25.799 --> 00:28:31.620
You're picking up on something, in my opinion,
equally if not more important, but I'm hopelessly

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:32.620
biased.

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:37.899
So for a little bit of quick context, I teach
in the undergraduate program in bioengineering

00:28:37.899 --> 00:28:38.899
at Stanford.

00:28:38.899 --> 00:28:44.070
This is the newest academic department in
the Engineering School in about 50 years.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:49.249
So last time we started a program of the scale
of bioengineering it was called computer science.

00:28:49.249 --> 00:28:50.249
(Laughter.)

00:28:50.249 --> 00:28:55.499
And in the laboratory course we offer here-in
the laboratory course we offer to our bioengineering

00:28:55.499 --> 00:28:58.879
students, we have outlawed gene editing.

00:28:58.879 --> 00:29:03.830
We refuse to teach our students how to cut
and paste DNA with any of the techniques,

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:09.129
including CRISPR, because we consider that
to be a waste of their time because the opportunity

00:29:09.129 --> 00:29:10.520
cost is so high.

00:29:10.520 --> 00:29:14.970
Instead, what we wish our students to learn
how to become better at is choosing what to

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:20.779
work on, designing a living organism, contracting
somebody else to manufacture that organism

00:29:20.779 --> 00:29:25.370
for then, and then receiving that and seeing
if it worked.

00:29:25.370 --> 00:29:30.229
So in other words, we decouple design and
fabrication, like an architect might design

00:29:30.229 --> 00:29:33.360
a building, a contractor will realize that
design for you.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:37.029
And so we've pioneered that in practice over
the last six years.

00:29:37.029 --> 00:29:42.489
For that to work practically-this gets back
to your framing of your question-a new technology

00:29:42.489 --> 00:29:44.479
needs to be made quite practical.

00:29:44.479 --> 00:29:46.940
It's called DNA synthesis.

00:29:46.940 --> 00:29:52.409
So can I go from information on a computer,
the letters of DNA-A, T, C, G-in whatever

00:29:52.409 --> 00:29:57.700
combination I want, send that information
over the internet to a foundry that will take

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:03.659
that string of information and print from
scratch the physical DNA molecule, and return

00:30:03.659 --> 00:30:09.149
that molecule to me either in a cell, or for
me to put in a cell, so that the genetic instructions

00:30:09.149 --> 00:30:11.350
are then carried out?

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:16.249
So when I first started teaching at MIT in
2003, the cost of going through this process,

00:30:16.249 --> 00:30:22.960
of paying somebody to print DNA for me, cost
$4 for every letter or base pair.

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:28.159
So if I had a gene that was a thousand bases
of DNA long that a student wanted, I'd have

00:30:28.159 --> 00:30:33.409
to give the company $4,000 to print that gene
from scratch so that the student wouldn't

00:30:33.409 --> 00:30:36.529
have to clone it or splice it or whatever.

00:30:36.529 --> 00:30:41.009
George and I have been involved with starting
two companies.

00:30:41.009 --> 00:30:49.700
And as a result, and due to the work of other
corporations as well, over the last 13 years

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:55.659
the price of printing DNA from scratch for
long, blank fragments has dropped by a factor

00:30:55.659 --> 00:30:56.659
of 400.

00:30:56.659 --> 00:31:02.740
So today, the cost of printing DNA from scratch
for the things we want is not $4 a letter,

00:31:02.740 --> 00:31:05.299
it's one penny a letter.

00:31:05.299 --> 00:31:11.279
And that continues to look like it will cost
down by about a factor of two every other

00:31:11.279 --> 00:31:14.169
year, for the next 10 years or so.

00:31:14.169 --> 00:31:18.249
GARRETT: It's faster than Murphy's-it's faster
than Murphy's Law.

00:31:18.249 --> 00:31:19.249
NICHOLS: Moore's.

00:31:19.249 --> 00:31:22.399
GARRETT: Moore's Law, I mean.

00:31:22.399 --> 00:31:24.999
Murphy's-(laughs)-god help us, that's biosafety.

00:31:24.999 --> 00:31:27.600
NICHOLS: That's a different problem.

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:32.239
ENDY: So we love this technology as would-be
engineers of biology, because it allows us

00:31:32.239 --> 00:31:36.960
to specialize on thinking what would be a
good thing to make and then, you know, getting

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:38.109
it made professionally.

00:31:38.109 --> 00:31:43.110
NICHOLS: Give us an example, Drew-give us
an example, Drew, of what you're making now.

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:48.919
ENDY: Well, the earlier example I started
with was making pain medications, right?

00:31:48.919 --> 00:31:55.630
So the teams-the teams who are programming
the baker's yeast are not, by themselves,

00:31:55.630 --> 00:32:01.440
going and starting with the plant materials
to harvest the genes and then cutting and

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:03.359
pasting them into yeast.

00:32:03.359 --> 00:32:07.499
They're starting with computer databases of
sequence information that are available over

00:32:07.499 --> 00:32:10.350
the internet, based in Canada or China or
wherever.

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:14.869
They're using computer algorithms to find
the putative sequences that they suspect will

00:32:14.869 --> 00:32:16.740
help do the biochemistry they want.

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:20.850
They're paying a company to print the DNA
from scratch and mail order it, you know,

00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:25.769
through FedEx to the lab at Stanford, and
instantiating those synthetic genetic programs

00:32:25.769 --> 00:32:28.669
into the organism, and off it goes.

00:32:28.669 --> 00:32:33.179
But there's many things you could do because
think about what biology can do, right?

00:32:33.179 --> 00:32:39.279
So it's a massively functional green nanotechnology
that's already taken over the planet.

00:32:39.279 --> 00:32:44.820
And to the extent you can ship information
or instructions, you know, via the internet

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:50.499
and overnight shipping, you can instantiate
manufacturing of new things wherever you wish,

00:32:50.499 --> 00:32:52.570
so long as you can operate the system.

00:32:52.570 --> 00:32:57.019
Now, to just return to, Laurie, your comment.

00:32:57.019 --> 00:33:02.419
Because the prices have been dropping faster
than computing has been improving, it's now

00:33:02.419 --> 00:33:09.799
become imaginable to finance a project to
synthesize all the DNA encoding a human being.

00:33:09.799 --> 00:33:14.629
So the human genome is about 3 billion bases
long, plus or minus.

00:33:14.629 --> 00:33:20.640
And if you divided that by a hundred at a
penny a base you would get $30 million as

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:24.169
the initial cost to print the DNA encoding
a human being.

00:33:24.169 --> 00:33:27.220
Now, that would not be a continuous genome.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:31.190
It would be in short chunks of 4(,000) to
10,000 bases, and you'd have to figure out

00:33:31.190 --> 00:33:33.080
how to physically assemble it.

00:33:33.080 --> 00:33:38.029
And so a meeting was held to discuss this,
as have other meetings.

00:33:38.029 --> 00:33:43.220
And there's now a proposal to underwrite and
pursue the-for the first time, development

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:47.230
of capacities to print from scratch and assemble
human genomes.

00:33:47.230 --> 00:33:49.690
GARRETT: And that meeting was secret.

00:33:49.690 --> 00:33:50.690
(Laughter.)

00:33:50.690 --> 00:33:52.529
GERBERDING: Obviously not.

00:33:52.529 --> 00:33:55.479
GARRETT: After the fact we all heard about
it.

00:33:55.479 --> 00:33:59.710
NICHOLS: We agreed on a conference call yesterday,
among the four of us, that this is not a good

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:02.479
idea to have a secret meeting, actually.

00:34:02.479 --> 00:34:08.010
Science thrives best when it's very open,
very transparent, very much debated.

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.150
And I think we've all agreed on that.

00:34:10.150 --> 00:34:16.580
Before we go to comments from our colleagues
here, what are any of the military implications

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:17.580
of this?

00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:21.730
The Council on Foreign Relations is concerned
with foreign relations and military relations.

00:34:21.730 --> 00:34:26.920
Are any of you worried about the peril of
this falling into the wrong hands and leading

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:27.920
to weapons?

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:29.790
GARRETT: Julie do you want to start?

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:30.790
NICHOLS: Julie?

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:34.720
GERBERDING: Yeah, falling into the wrong hands
I think is probably not an applicable concept

00:34:34.720 --> 00:34:38.310
here since, you know, a six-grader can probably
use these technologies.

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:42.100
So we should assume that everybody has the
capability.

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:44.400
The question is-
NICHOLS: But that's a significant statement

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:45.400
in and of itself.

00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:46.400
It's ubiquitous.

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:47.400
GERBERDING: Exactly.

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:52.310
Which is, I'm sure, why this is on the list
of WMDs that our national security advisor

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:53.810
just created.

00:34:53.810 --> 00:35:03.400
But you know, it's like the concern about
any biological threat.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:08.400
Just because you can do it doesn't mean it's
a weapon of choice, doesn't mean it would

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:12.030
be the most terrifying weapon of choice.

00:35:12.030 --> 00:35:15.350
Mother Nature is a very good terrorist herself.

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:19.180
But I certainly think it deserves consideration
in that paradigm.

00:35:19.180 --> 00:35:20.710
NICHOLS: Mmm hmm.

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:22.940
GARRETT: I am less concerned-
ENDY: Could I just-sorry.

00:35:22.940 --> 00:35:23.940
GARRETT: Oh, sorry, Drew.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:25.050
Do you want to go?

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:26.960
ENDY: No, please go ahead, Laurie.

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:27.960
GARRETT: OK.

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:38.100
I'm less concerned about deliberate use, deliberate
modification, compared to accidental and biosafety

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:39.100
concerns.

00:35:39.100 --> 00:35:43.410
You know, Julie used to run the CDC.

00:35:43.410 --> 00:35:51.600
And in the era after she left, we've seen,
what, eight major incidents of biosafety breaches?

00:35:51.600 --> 00:35:56.280
And that's at the Centers for Disease Control
and Prevention, our most security civilian

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:58.350
facility for biological research.

00:35:58.350 --> 00:35:59.350
NICHOLS: Accidents happen.

00:35:59.350 --> 00:36:00.350
Accidents happen.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:01.350
GARRETT: Accidents happen.

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:09.840
And when you get to the point where it's kids
in high school labs, or, you know, mentally

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:16.290
confused college kids playing around and imagining
they're the next "Breaking Bad" mad scientist,

00:36:16.290 --> 00:36:21.540
or what have you, then I start getting very
worried and very concerned about whether or

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:26.150
not we've really thought through how to create
a safety paradigm.

00:36:26.150 --> 00:36:31.140
And you know, when Drew described this whole
new algorithm of how people are approaching

00:36:31.140 --> 00:36:39.420
biology, kind of following a trajectory of
the placement of creativity versus distributed

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:48.720
production and so on that has made the tech
and computer revolution, you know, look at

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:51.110
what has resulted on the computer side.

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:57.220
We've gone from outsourcing more and more
and more of computer production and chip production

00:36:57.220 --> 00:37:03.110
and smartphones and everything to China and
to Russia, to a lesser degree.

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:09.490
And now we have this massive cybersecurity
crisis, where nobody in the world feels like

00:37:09.490 --> 00:37:16.730
their data is secure, that their very personality,
their very thoughts are accessible, whether

00:37:16.730 --> 00:37:21.720
it's to their own government or to some hacker
somewhere out there, or to a foreign government.

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:26.780
And so we're squarely in the realm of all
aspects national security.

00:37:26.780 --> 00:37:33.850
And I think, you know, this biology revolution
is taking the genome to the place where the

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:36.060
cyber has been.

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:41.250
It'll have different dimensions, but it's
similarly so diffuse and distributed across

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:46.230
so many national boundaries and involves so
many different legal systems that essentially

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:47.960
it's beyond regulation.

00:37:47.960 --> 00:37:49.040
NICHOLS: Mmm hmm.

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:55.570
Drew, you wanted to make a point on the military-potential
military applications or foreign policy implications?

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:56.630
ENDY: Sure.

00:37:56.630 --> 00:37:58.400
It just-it's a retro comment.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:04.670
The last time I was at the Council I think
was joined with Matt Meselson.

00:38:04.670 --> 00:38:10.330
And one of the important things to remember,
it's slightly before my time, is, you know,

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:15.860
we in the U.S. used to have an offensive biological
weapons program and made a very good decision

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:19.090
heading into the Nixon administration to stand
that down.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:23.850
And so it's just worth remembering that and
bringing that wisdom forward.

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:30.290
We should now, in my opinion, take actions
that even inadvertently might lead others

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:32.630
to remilitarize biology.

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:37.640
So although we don't say it out loud very
much, it is absolutely critical that nation-states

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:42.980
do not remilitarize biology on top of the
new capacities that are developed.

00:38:42.980 --> 00:38:47.390
And it's a retro comment, because we worked
it through many decades ago, but let's not

00:38:47.390 --> 00:38:48.390
lose that one.

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:49.390
NICHOLS: Yeah.

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:50.430
A norm was set.

00:38:50.430 --> 00:38:54.670
And I think one of the things we were talking
about earlier, there are few norms here in

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:55.670
this business.

00:38:55.670 --> 00:39:00.750
GARRETT: Well, there was a norm set, but to
Drew's point, it was massively violated.

00:39:00.750 --> 00:39:05.720
I mean, there was a treaty signed, it was
in the Nixon administration, everybody was

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:09.850
getting rid of their bioweapons, and that's
when the Soviets, in fact, revved their entire

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:17.670
program up so that they had this massive biological
weapons program, that included use of primitive

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:20.740
genetic engineering to enhance-
NICHOLS: Which was secret.

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:24.530
Which was secret and very often doubted whether
they really had it at the time.

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:25.530
GARRETT: Yes.

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:28.680
NICHOLS: We want to open it up to all of you
for comments and questions.

00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:31.400
I think there are some mics roaming around.

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:38.220
The question in the center here, he's got
his card up.

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:40.010
Right in the center.

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:41.010
Could you identify yourself?

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:42.010
Q: Thank you.

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:43.010
Yes, I shall.

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:44.010
Richard Huber.

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:47.020
I'm the chairman of a company called InVina
Wine.

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:56.000
But I'm here really as an investor and a director
of AquaBounty, a company that has got some

00:39:56.000 --> 00:40:02.310
notoriety for having the first genetically
modified animal product that has been approved.

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:07.840
And my question is related to that, and you've
all touched upon it.

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:15.730
The U.S. regulatory process, having seen it
close hand, 13 years to get approval from

00:40:15.730 --> 00:40:18.970
the FDA, is a shambles.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:26.170
And supposedly science-based regulatory is
a myth.

00:40:26.170 --> 00:40:29.350
Politics is all over the place.

00:40:29.350 --> 00:40:36.540
Can you suggest some better way of having
a regulatory framework that both provides

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:43.110
protection-something you commented on many
times-but also allows the process to move

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:46.610
at a somewhat faster pace than 13 years?

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:47.610
Thank you.

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:50.090
NICHOLS: That's a tough question, Mr. Huber,
I think.

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:51.860
And I wish Peggy Hamburg were here.

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:55.750
I'm sure she didn't-she didn't block your
product, I'm sure.

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:59.950
GERBERDING: I don't know what Peggy would
say, and I certainly don't intend to speak

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:06.660
for her, but I am part of the bio-innovation
trade association executive committee.

00:41:06.660 --> 00:41:12.370
And of course, we're very keen to look at
how the FDA can have what it needs to be able

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:13.910
to improve its performance.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:16.520
And speed is one of those performance metrics.

00:41:16.520 --> 00:41:20.270
So I believe the agency wants to improve.

00:41:20.270 --> 00:41:24.740
The 21st century cures legislation that's
passed the House but not the Senate is an

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:27.500
important step in that direction.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:31.640
But there's also the missing underpinning
of regulatory science.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:35.790
And when we think about scientific investments
in the United States, I think we zoom to the

00:41:35.790 --> 00:41:38.830
NIH or maybe the National Academy of Science.

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:43.890
But we don't necessarily think about the surround
sound that has to go on to make those scientific

00:41:43.890 --> 00:41:46.550
advances available to people.

00:41:46.550 --> 00:41:48.950
And regulatory science is a big piece of that.

00:41:48.950 --> 00:41:53.840
So what we're really talking about today is
a prime example of where we're going to need

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:59.490
a whole new framework for regulatory science
to be able to do what we all want to do, which

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:03.250
is accelerate the value of the innovations
that we're creating.

00:42:03.250 --> 00:42:08.150
NICHOLS: I also think the staffing at FDA
has been a problem for Peggy Hamburg and her

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:09.150
predecessors.

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:14.230
There are not very many first-rate molecular
biologists and geneticists who'd rather work

00:42:14.230 --> 00:42:16.790
for the FDA than Stanford, or even CDC.

00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:18.060
GARRETT: Why would they?

00:42:18.060 --> 00:42:19.970
I mean, look, let's been real.

00:42:19.970 --> 00:42:25.670
It's just like trying to get top of the line
cyber folks working for the FBI.

00:42:25.670 --> 00:42:33.720
Why would you want to take a salary of $90,000
a year, or $110,000 a year, with all the government

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:40.290
bureaucracy you have to live with, when you
could make 10 times with a smart play crossing

00:42:40.290 --> 00:42:41.720
between academia and the private sector.

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:45.580
GERBERDING: But, Laurie, some of us do it
because we care and we think it's the right

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:47.250
thing to do and, you know, we want to serve
our country.

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:48.510
GARRETT: Oh, right, but, you know, that's
the minority.

00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:49.510
That's a distinct minority.

00:42:49.510 --> 00:42:53.000
GERBERDING: But I do think that-you know,
I want to very respectful of the quality of

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:57.540
the scientists that are at the FDA, because
there are some very outstanding people there.

00:42:57.540 --> 00:43:04.240
And again, part of the 21st century cure legislation
is to give the FDA the hiring flexibilities

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:07.820
and the salary support that they need to try
to be more attractive to people.

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:12.090
So I really want to get behind them and say
how can we help you, because I completely

00:43:12.090 --> 00:43:13.160
agree with your point.

00:43:13.160 --> 00:43:17.160
We all agree with the problem that we need
to help them solve it.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:18.160
NICHOLS: Another question?

00:43:18.160 --> 00:43:19.160
Yes, here, on the right.

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:21.550
Yes, sir, your name and affiliation, please.

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:23.290
Q: John Chain (sp).

00:43:23.290 --> 00:43:27.580
I'm a columnist, and I do other things.

00:43:27.580 --> 00:43:33.200
Do we know anything at all about what's happening
at Biopreparat, the Russian militarization

00:43:33.200 --> 00:43:34.400
of bio?

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:37.660
GARRETT: That's been long-since destroyed,
Biopreparat.

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:43.640
What we don't know-so, under the Soviets there
were two giant arms of biological weapons

00:43:43.640 --> 00:43:44.640
production.

00:43:44.640 --> 00:43:47.430
One was Biopreparat, which was allegedly civilian.

00:43:47.430 --> 00:43:51.270
It answered directly to the Politburo.

00:43:51.270 --> 00:43:54.870
And the other was what was going on inside
the military.

00:43:54.870 --> 00:43:57.960
Biopreparat became transparent.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:06.020
There were many years of program with our
National Academy of Sciences to transform

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:12.580
the entire infrastructure to public health
good and find employment for those scientists

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:15.660
outside of weapons production.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:20.930
What we don't know almost anything about is
what really was going on inside the military

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:25.830
labs, and what may or may not persist in Russian
military labs.

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:27.290
That we don't know.

00:44:27.290 --> 00:44:30.500
NICHOLS: So you can't write a column about
a secret.

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:32.590
We don't know the answer.

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:33.640
Another question or comment?

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:35.670
Yes, sir, over here on the left.

00:44:35.670 --> 00:44:38.200
Your name and affiliation, please.

00:44:38.200 --> 00:44:41.490
Q: Stanley Arkin, I'm a member of the Council.

00:44:41.490 --> 00:44:49.840
My son, a professor, works at Cal in bioengineering,
synthetic biology, Adam Arkin.

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:50.860
And you may know him.

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:54.000
ENDY: A very dear friend and colleague, yes.

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:59.640
Q: And from time to time when he speaks to
me about the issue, what's occurred to me

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:02.520
is at what point does security attach?

00:45:02.520 --> 00:45:07.350
He's given me hypotheticals which would curl
your hair.

00:45:07.350 --> 00:45:10.310
At what point-is it an outside governmental
agency?

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:15.490
Does the university have some kind of special
authority or obligation?

00:45:15.490 --> 00:45:23.560
But at what point in the course of research
does security intervene and therefore seek

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:24.920
to protect us all?

00:45:24.920 --> 00:45:29.451
NICHOLS: Drew, I think that was directed at
you, but other panelists may want to make

00:45:29.451 --> 00:45:30.750
a quick comment too.

00:45:30.750 --> 00:45:32.350
ENDY: Sure.

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:37.230
So a pleasure to meet you through the video
link, sir.

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:42.510
Adam is a dear friend, and an amazing person.

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:48.590
I think it depends on the aspect of security
specifically that arises.

00:45:48.590 --> 00:45:54.610
So you know, there's not a blanket statement
that is responsive to anything-everything

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:56.290
that could be practiced.

00:45:56.290 --> 00:46:03.270
One of the hallmarks of how modern biotechnology
flourishes is that there is a distribution

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:10.010
of responsibility to the nodes, to the community,
to the protagonist, to the individual actors.

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:19.330
And so in the just conceiving of an idea,
a good citizen is mindful of safety and security

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:20.400
aspects.

00:46:20.400 --> 00:46:31.140
And to attempt to implement, you know, only
a top-down control framework doesn't scale.

00:46:31.140 --> 00:46:38.120
There are exceptions to this, which have been
developed, in my opinion, on a responsive

00:46:38.120 --> 00:46:39.430
and ad hoc basis.

00:46:39.430 --> 00:46:45.520
You know, so for example spent more time over
the last 20 months than I might have wished

00:46:45.520 --> 00:46:49.790
as a member of the National Biosecurity Board,
trying to be responsive to government requests

00:46:49.790 --> 00:46:57.630
around how-if and how work involving gain-of-function
with flu and MERS and SARS might proceed or

00:46:57.630 --> 00:46:58.700
not.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:02.790
And about a month ago, we shipped our recommendations
into the government for how to think about-

00:47:02.790 --> 00:47:06.490
NICHOLS: You better explain a little more
of what that means, what the problem was.

00:47:06.490 --> 00:47:08.370
But take a minute or two to.

00:47:08.370 --> 00:47:09.370
ENDY: Sure.

00:47:09.370 --> 00:47:15.200
But I mean, but it's a good example to illustrate
how much we're on our heels in many respects

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:17.860
in terms of responding to security concerns.

00:47:17.860 --> 00:47:23.290
So a number of years ago, as many might know,
there was public funding for work to explore

00:47:23.290 --> 00:47:32.700
how particular strains of flu might be adapted
to potentially create a new type of flu variant

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:34.260
that might lead to a pandemic.

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:39.730
So, could you take bird flu and have it be
adapted so it would passage through mammals,

00:47:39.730 --> 00:47:42.220
create casualties in mammals.

00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:43.550
Very controversial work.

00:47:43.550 --> 00:47:48.790
When it was brought forward by the research
community, it first emerged in the context

00:47:48.790 --> 00:47:53.560
of scientific papers that were being considered
for publication.

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:58.770
And at that point, very late in the practice
of the work, concerns were raised that resulted

00:47:58.770 --> 00:48:04.000
in debate about whether or not such research
should be published ever, never mind whether

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:06.370
or not the work should have been done.

00:48:06.370 --> 00:48:13.090
This experience, combined with imperfect inventory
control, imperfect management of shipping

00:48:13.090 --> 00:48:19.320
of samples from U.S. government labs, combined
with other factors, led to the federal government

00:48:19.320 --> 00:48:27.290
putting a pause on all funding for doing gain-of-function
research on specific human pathogens-viral

00:48:27.290 --> 00:48:29.260
pathogens, like flu.

00:48:29.260 --> 00:48:34.470
And it took us the better part of 20 months
to work through the aspects of that and come

00:48:34.470 --> 00:48:39.550
up with a set of recommendations that the
federal government could consider implementing

00:48:39.550 --> 00:48:43.520
in terms of if and how to allow such work
to proceed.

00:48:43.520 --> 00:48:47.270
The point I'm trying to communicate, if I
zoom out for a second, is this is a lot of

00:48:47.270 --> 00:48:49.760
work for a specific case.

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:52.040
We're operating on our heels a little bit.

00:48:52.040 --> 00:48:54.420
It's very ad hoc and idiosyncratic.

00:48:54.420 --> 00:48:57.110
It doesn't really scale as an approach.

00:48:57.110 --> 00:49:02.960
And if you look at where this landscape is
going, and how the technology trends are moving,

00:49:02.960 --> 00:49:09.490
it seems like we have a lot more on the horizon
and beyond the horizon to consider.

00:49:09.490 --> 00:49:13.880
So I'll say something-I'll say something that
sounds a little bit strange, but one of the-if

00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:18.590
I live in the future, where we've made living
matter fully engineerable, and we then ask

00:49:18.590 --> 00:49:23.470
how do we wish to flourish, how are we going
to manage safety and security, what our plan?

00:49:23.470 --> 00:49:28.140
Safety, I think, is both practice and culture.

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:30.000
Security is a little bit different.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:34.810
But the lessons I look to come out of Ohio
in the late 1960s.

00:49:34.810 --> 00:49:41.230
So the Supreme Court cases, Jacobellis versus
Ohio, having to do with obscenity and speech,

00:49:41.230 --> 00:49:46.810
Brandenburg versus Ohio, having to do with
inciting lawlessness, and how Justice Potter

00:49:46.810 --> 00:49:53.070
Stewart and others rendered opinions that
recognized that they could not, in a top-down

00:49:53.070 --> 00:50:00.030
fashion, prescribe whether or not a particular
work was obscene or was likely to incite lawlessness

00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:01.740
and intending to do so.

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:07.680
The wisdom of those two court decisions is
they recognized the open-ended, generative

00:50:07.680 --> 00:50:09.430
nature of speech.

00:50:09.430 --> 00:50:14.480
And I think what we're looking at in biology
is a tremendous open-ended capacity to be

00:50:14.480 --> 00:50:16.290
constructive and creative.

00:50:16.290 --> 00:50:20.920
We have to create a culture and a practice,
and promulgate a culture and a practice, that

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:28.140
I believe would lead to overwhelming practice
of constructive behavior, and a technical

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:34.020
and public health capacity to respond when
the practice is accidentally harmful, or when

00:50:34.020 --> 00:50:35.310
we have mal-intentioned actors.

00:50:35.310 --> 00:50:37.570
And it's an-
GARRETT: If I may-

00:50:37.570 --> 00:50:42.110
NICHOLS: Let's get another comment-we'll come
back, Laurie-another comment of question.

00:50:42.110 --> 00:50:45.560
Yes, sir, in the center.

00:50:45.560 --> 00:50:49.030
Q: Jeffrey Glueck from Foursquare.

00:50:49.030 --> 00:50:51.980
A question for Professor Endy and one for
Laurie.

00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:56.550
Is it possible that you could engineer a gene
drive, professor, that you could insert into

00:50:56.550 --> 00:51:03.550
a nation's food supply, that would rend the
entire food supply vulnerable to a trigger

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:10.560
chemical or the like, as a bioweapon, or engineer
an insect to bite humans and insert a gene

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:11.560
drive into humans?

00:51:11.560 --> 00:51:15.240
Are these-are these conceivable bioweapons
with the new gene drive technology?

00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:20.150
And for Laurie, the U.S. is signatory to the
ENMOD, the Environmental Modifications Treaty,

00:51:20.150 --> 00:51:21.700
banning environmental modifications.

00:51:21.700 --> 00:51:27.200
Should that global entity be convened as a
forum for some of these global regulations?

00:51:27.200 --> 00:51:34.150
NICHOLS: So the first question was to Drew,
I think, gene drive in the food supply.

00:51:34.150 --> 00:51:37.410
ENDY: Yeah, very quickly-yeah, very quickly,
we don't know but it's conceivable.

00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:41.000
The patent filings coming out of Harvard suggest
that that could be practiced, but nobody's

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:42.250
practiced it.

00:51:42.250 --> 00:51:46.140
I'd refer to Kevin Esvelt and others for thoughtful,
technical responses.

00:51:46.140 --> 00:51:49.990
The one thing I would point out regarding
gene drives is I have not seen any experimental

00:51:49.990 --> 00:51:55.620
evidence of a gene drive been 100 percent
successful in terms of penetrating a population,

00:51:55.620 --> 00:52:00.130
even in the most carefully controlled laboratory
experiments with relatively homogeneous strains

00:52:00.130 --> 00:52:01.130
of yeast.

00:52:01.130 --> 00:52:04.890
You get to the high 90s, but I've never seen
anything to total completion.

00:52:04.890 --> 00:52:09.530
Now, I might not have the latest data, but
I think that's relevant from a policy perspective.

00:52:09.530 --> 00:52:12.850
Could you still nevertheless realize disturbances
of some significance?

00:52:12.850 --> 00:52:13.850
Yes.

00:52:13.850 --> 00:52:19.060
And then just as a placeholder, your second
question, you know, implies, in a way, being

00:52:19.060 --> 00:52:23.930
responsive to some of the comments coming
from the ETC Group and others, in terms of

00:52:23.930 --> 00:52:28.050
how one might want to consider the regulation
of gene drives.

00:52:28.050 --> 00:52:31.610
So I can point you to that offline, if you
like.

00:52:31.610 --> 00:52:32.610
NICHOLS: Laurie.

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:33.610
GARRETT: Yeah.

00:52:33.610 --> 00:52:40.050
I think I'd kind of prefer to take it jumping
off the previous question, because it doesn't

00:52:40.050 --> 00:52:47.100
matter which international entity you imagine
activating to address which specific problem.

00:52:47.100 --> 00:52:52.380
We have a first problem in front of that,
which Drew hinted at when he said, you know,

00:52:52.380 --> 00:52:55.990
safety and security are cultural issues.

00:52:55.990 --> 00:52:58.330
They're about the culture of how you do science.

00:52:58.330 --> 00:53:03.670
and they're about the culture in which science
is occurring-what's going on around the outside

00:53:03.670 --> 00:53:04.730
of it.

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:09.330
It's about the nature of governance in any
given setting-whether it's how a university

00:53:09.330 --> 00:53:16.250
governs safety on campus of how a nation governs
safety within and without its borders.

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:22.640
And when we've had various international-level
meetings looking at GOF, looking at DURC,

00:53:22.640 --> 00:53:28.050
and now looking at synthetic biology questions,
what you see is that the world cannot even

00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:31.880
agree on the word security, OK?

00:53:31.880 --> 00:53:33.540
And it's very serious.

00:53:33.540 --> 00:53:40.870
I see it now also in trying to improve the
World Health Organization so that it's capable

00:53:40.870 --> 00:53:45.870
of responding better than it did to Ebola
to the next great outbreak, which now the

00:53:45.870 --> 00:53:49.470
next great is already here, it's called Zika.

00:53:49.470 --> 00:53:55.840
And what you see is that the word "security"
is so charged, it's perceived when Americans

00:53:55.840 --> 00:54:02.850
say the term, as we're about security for
us not you.

00:54:02.850 --> 00:54:06.740
But we want you to do things so that we feel
safer.

00:54:06.740 --> 00:54:12.690
And while we're at it, we define security
blended in with terrorism.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:20.280
And if you go to poor countries, particularly
in Africa and South Asia, their attitude is:

00:54:20.280 --> 00:54:25.610
You're just trying to come in here and mobilize
us, and get us to buy into your tech and your

00:54:25.610 --> 00:54:28.820
parameters of control.

00:54:28.820 --> 00:54:31.890
But it's all in your interests, not ours.

00:54:31.890 --> 00:54:37.440
And one of the biggest complaints you hear
at a lot of meetings on this topic is that

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:45.720
the more you securitize biology, the harder
it is for people from many parts of the world

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:52.830
to engage with biological enterprises in Western
Europe and North America, because they become

00:54:52.830 --> 00:54:54.350
security risks.

00:54:54.350 --> 00:55:00.240
So if you're a really smart 18-year old in
Afghanistan who knows a lot, and is super

00:55:00.240 --> 00:55:04.810
precocious, and ready to take on the next
great CRISPR revolution, it's a lot harder

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:12.140
for you to get clearance to come to Stanford
compared to, say, a kid from Shenzhen or a

00:55:12.140 --> 00:55:14.570
kid from Palo Alto.

00:55:14.570 --> 00:55:20.560
So I think, you know, when we activate these
words and think about the policy implications

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:27.380
of them, it's incumbent on Americans to step
back and see how is this being perceived by

00:55:27.380 --> 00:55:29.230
the non-American?

00:55:29.230 --> 00:55:35.000
What are we projecting as our notion of security
and safety?

00:55:35.000 --> 00:55:36.540
NICHOLS: Thank you.

00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:37.540
Another comment?

00:55:37.540 --> 00:55:41.410
Let's see, over there on the left here.

00:55:41.410 --> 00:55:43.070
Yes, sir.

00:55:43.070 --> 00:55:44.070
Yeah.

00:55:44.070 --> 00:55:46.410
Q: Thank you.

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:47.410
Exceptional discussion.

00:55:47.410 --> 00:55:50.520
Earl Carr, representing Momentum Advisors.

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:56.700
What would it take to get countries like China,
Russia, to collaborate more with the scientific

00:55:56.700 --> 00:55:58.370
community to focus on safety?

00:55:58.370 --> 00:56:03.480
And in particular when I say countries, I'm
specifically referring to governments.

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:06.020
NICHOLS: That's a tough one.

00:56:06.020 --> 00:56:10.620
Julie, you've done a lot of international
work, both at CDC and now at Merck.

00:56:10.620 --> 00:56:14.200
What's your-
GERBERDING: Well, it's a really hard question

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:20.550
to answer today in this kind of ultra-nationalistic
wave that we're experiencing.

00:56:20.550 --> 00:56:26.400
So I'm not sure that we have the mechanisms
to reliably do that.

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:29.240
And we were just talking about culture.

00:56:29.240 --> 00:56:35.930
So what is the interest of the country in
this technology and what is the culture and

00:56:35.930 --> 00:56:41.410
the spirit of collaboration and biosecurity
in that culture?

00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:49.080
So of course, I think relationships matter
and global norms matter, global-above national

00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:50.900
agencies should matter.

00:56:50.900 --> 00:56:57.690
I wish we had a WHO that was capable and confident
to provide that kind of global leadership.

00:56:57.690 --> 00:57:01.510
And I think it's something we could talk about
at another panel someday.

00:57:01.510 --> 00:57:07.230
But, you know, it's a very sobering question,
because I don't think we have the ability

00:57:07.230 --> 00:57:12.100
to do that, nor do we have it with any other
technology, such as cybersecurity, or any

00:57:12.100 --> 00:57:15.000
of the other global threats that we're experiencing.

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:17.200
So I don't see this as a unique problem.

00:57:17.200 --> 00:57:23.050
I just see it as a problem that is part of
the world-the flat world in which we're living.

00:57:23.050 --> 00:57:29.280
GARRETT: I think you need to separate the
governments from everybody else, because actually

00:57:29.280 --> 00:57:35.560
there's very, very aggressive collaboration
going on right now, particularly between Americans,

00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:37.010
Europeans and Chinese.

00:57:37.010 --> 00:57:44.150
China is, you know, the biggest sequencer
on the planet, and the biggest databases-although

00:57:44.150 --> 00:57:46.520
I don't know if Drew agrees with me.

00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:51.060
But I think the largest databases on genetic
sequences are all pretty much in China right

00:57:51.060 --> 00:57:52.060
now.

00:57:52.060 --> 00:57:57.460
But when you talk about governments, that's
a whole other kettle of fish.

00:57:57.460 --> 00:58:00.200
Two problems always come forward.

00:58:00.200 --> 00:58:06.730
First of all, government, as a thoughtful
organism, or whatever the heck it is, is always

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:08.460
way behind.

00:58:08.460 --> 00:58:13.770
So the science is over here, and government's
back here arguing-we're still really arguing

00:58:13.770 --> 00:58:22.590
20th century paradigms in a lot of the sort
of collective international debate about regulation-whether

00:58:22.590 --> 00:58:29.280
it's about regulating counterfeit drugs in
pharmaceuticals, or regulating how you would

00:58:29.280 --> 00:58:33.360
implement various types of synthetic biology.

00:58:33.360 --> 00:58:35.190
So government is always slow.

00:58:35.190 --> 00:58:41.160
And then just to the cultural thing, one anecdote
I think is worth remembering.

00:58:41.160 --> 00:58:45.380
We didn't-it was previously brought up, the
question about Biopreparat.

00:58:45.380 --> 00:58:52.430
So the only way the West even knew Biopreparat
existed was a defector gave the information

00:58:52.430 --> 00:58:57.200
to the British government, and Margaret Thatcher
confronted Boris Yeltsin.

00:58:57.200 --> 00:59:00.010
So this is after the fall of the Soviet Union.

00:59:00.010 --> 00:59:06.420
It's still going on in Russia, in Kazakhstan,
in a few other places.

00:59:06.420 --> 00:59:15.550
And in that confrontation, she is informed,
well, when Nixon signed the agreement with

00:59:15.550 --> 00:59:23.210
Brezhnev eliminating biological weapons, Brezhnev
went running back to Moscow and said: The

00:59:23.210 --> 00:59:27.600
Americans must have a huge bioweapons program
because they just had us sign this.

00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:30.000
So let's get going, right?

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:31.000
(Laughter.)

00:59:31.000 --> 00:59:37.600
So that's-remember, government is based on
its politics, its ideology, and its culture.

00:59:37.600 --> 00:59:40.780
And not every government is going to see this
problem the same way.

00:59:40.780 --> 00:59:45.420
NICHOLS: A further footnote on your-this last
question is there are science advisors now

00:59:45.420 --> 00:59:48.460
to prime ministers and presidents around the
world.

00:59:48.460 --> 00:59:53.400
And very often, some good ministers have helped,
as Julie and I were discussing, as I'm sure

00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:54.400
Laurie knows.

00:59:54.400 --> 00:59:59.410
So there is intellectual capacity in a lot
of governments, but it has to deal with all

00:59:59.410 --> 01:00:04.420
these political and cultural and ideological
barriers before that intellectual capacity

01:00:04.420 --> 01:00:07.520
can get deployed in the way that I think you
had in mind.

01:00:07.520 --> 01:00:11.870
GERBERDING: I mean, one thing I would just
add to that is that I sense that I'm in a

01:00:11.870 --> 01:00:18.380
room of elites, some really, really, really
brilliant people who have a breadth of knowledge

01:00:18.380 --> 01:00:20.980
and scientific literacy and a lot of capabilities.

01:00:20.980 --> 01:00:26.920
But, you know, talk about the 0.1 percent,
you know, we live in a world where scientific

01:00:26.920 --> 01:00:37.170
competency, numeracy, literacy is increasingly
the focus of enormous disparity.

01:00:37.170 --> 01:00:43.350
And so the people at the level who can take
in this kind of conversation and participate

01:00:43.350 --> 01:00:49.730
in it in the debate or the dialogue, versus
the citizenry of the nation, you know, that

01:00:49.730 --> 01:00:56.770
is becoming increasingly large chasm of-
NICHOLS: A gap.

01:00:56.770 --> 01:00:57.770
GERBERDING: A huge gap.

01:00:57.770 --> 01:01:04.290
And you know, we can't even count on citizens
to make good health decisions about ordinary

01:01:04.290 --> 01:01:10.060
things that are fairly easy to understand,
let alone debate the merits of CRISPR, gene

01:01:10.060 --> 01:01:11.060
drive, or gene editing.

01:01:11.060 --> 01:01:16.050
And what happens when people don't understand
science is they default to very black and

01:01:16.050 --> 01:01:17.470
white solutions.

01:01:17.470 --> 01:01:23.530
Like it's either really bad, no GMO, that's
off the table, or they just give up and, you

01:01:23.530 --> 01:01:28.740
know, don't pay attention and don't engage
in the kind of citizenry manner that would

01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:29.930
be helpful.

01:01:29.930 --> 01:01:37.310
So I think we have to accept the reality that
not only we do we have to deal with the environment-the

01:01:37.310 --> 01:01:41.520
safety and security environment of these technologies,
but we also have to deal with the fact that

01:01:41.520 --> 01:01:44.280
we really need to help people understand what
they are.

01:01:44.280 --> 01:01:45.950
And that's a tall order.

01:01:45.950 --> 01:01:49.580
NICHOLS: In the back, on the left, thank you.

01:01:49.580 --> 01:01:52.400
Q: David Bard, American Securities.

01:01:52.400 --> 01:01:58.510
Drew, early on you alluded to a lack of policy,
writ large.

01:01:58.510 --> 01:02:02.880
And you compared it to nuclear policy as that
is well-done and this not.

01:02:02.880 --> 01:02:07.740
We've hit on a lot of pieces of what that
policy might look like throughout this conversation,

01:02:07.740 --> 01:02:11.590
but could you and then maybe others lay out
what would the framework look like if you

01:02:11.590 --> 01:02:14.540
could do it and you didn't have a lot of these
constraints?

01:02:14.540 --> 01:02:18.280
And then what reading material would you suggest
for people who are total novices and don't

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:22.360
understand some of the acronyms that are sort
of flying around?

01:02:22.360 --> 01:02:25.930
NICHOLS: Drew, you want to take a crack at
that in 60 seconds?

01:02:25.930 --> 01:02:26.930
(Laughter.)

01:02:26.930 --> 01:02:27.930
ENDY: Sure.

01:02:27.930 --> 01:02:38.560
So to the second question, I would point you
and others to iGEM, yet another acronym, I-G-E-M.

01:02:38.560 --> 01:02:42.300
And if you go to iGEM.org on the web, you'll
see something remarkable.

01:02:42.300 --> 01:02:47.160
It's an International Genetically Engineered
Machines competition.

01:02:47.160 --> 01:02:50.670
It's a genetic engineering Olympics.

01:02:50.670 --> 01:02:55.660
It will take over the Hynes Convention Center
in Boston this fall, where you'll have about

01:02:55.660 --> 01:03:01.450
4,000 teenagers showing up with their constructions
or creations, their living organisms that

01:03:01.450 --> 01:03:03.390
they've made.

01:03:03.390 --> 01:03:09.000
The 300 teams, 40 countries, the most creative
up-and-coming biotechnology leaders on the

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:11.820
planet, to a first approximation.

01:03:11.820 --> 01:03:16.150
We have evidence now because there's 30,000
alumni from this program.

01:03:16.150 --> 01:03:21.920
And they're running many of the most interesting
start-up companies in biotechnology across

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:23.250
the planet.

01:03:23.250 --> 01:03:26.860
So I would just check out what the iGEM students
are doing.

01:03:26.860 --> 01:03:30.180
All their presentation videos are free to
watch online.

01:03:30.180 --> 01:03:36.790
And there's a tremendous resourcing of information
and materials through the iGEM website.

01:03:36.790 --> 01:03:42.869
If you wanted to promulgate a new standard
of safety or security or any sort of cultural

01:03:42.869 --> 01:03:48.900
practice globally, and actually deploy that
next year, one of the things you could do

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:52.140
is you could change the judging requirements
in iGEM.

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:56.660
And now, all of a sudden, 5,000 people around
the world would try and practice that to earn

01:03:56.660 --> 01:03:58.960
the medals in the competition.

01:03:58.960 --> 01:04:06.060
So a placeholder just policy comment, to come
back to the first question, is there's a tremendous

01:04:06.060 --> 01:04:08.740
amount of cultural or soft power.

01:04:08.740 --> 01:04:12.380
There's a tremendous amount of network effects
being established.

01:04:12.380 --> 01:04:17.220
And were we to be strategic about realizing
what we wished for, we would embrace those

01:04:17.220 --> 01:04:21.000
possibilities and figure out how to make the
world better.

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:27.390
I think Robbie Barbero at OSTP around the
topic of regulation, the gentleman from AquaBounty,

01:04:27.390 --> 01:04:33.040
you know, what Robbie has been trying to do,
and it's a struggle, is to basically implement

01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:41.420
a process wherein things are being considered
on an ongoing basis, not just the specific

01:04:41.420 --> 01:04:46.250
cases that are coming forward for review and
perhaps approval, but how the practice is

01:04:46.250 --> 01:04:51.619
being carried out, how the systems are working
or not working, how different agencies could

01:04:51.619 --> 01:04:52.720
do better.

01:04:52.720 --> 01:04:56.620
Are there gaps in the portfolio of agencies
or boards?

01:04:56.620 --> 01:05:02.500
I think that way of thinking, recognizing
that when you have a technology-a set of technologies

01:05:02.500 --> 01:05:08.020
in biology and biotech that are moving so
quickly, you have to sustain work to figure

01:05:08.020 --> 01:05:10.150
out what the plan should be, right?

01:05:10.150 --> 01:05:14.410
So when Gretzky describes he's a good hockey
player because he skates to where the puck

01:05:14.410 --> 01:05:19.130
is going to be, even with ice hockey there's
friction on the rink and the puck is slowing

01:05:19.130 --> 01:05:20.130
down.

01:05:20.130 --> 01:05:23.030
We've got a little rocket on the puck.

01:05:23.030 --> 01:05:24.880
The puck is accelerating.

01:05:24.880 --> 01:05:28.420
And we need to figure out how to skate to
where the puck is going to be still to be

01:05:28.420 --> 01:05:29.420
strategic.

01:05:29.420 --> 01:05:33.619
And so I'd like to see sustained schools of
scholarship.

01:05:33.619 --> 01:05:36.560
I'd like to see sustained debates among the
agencies.

01:05:36.560 --> 01:05:39.410
I'd like to see sustained investments.

01:05:39.410 --> 01:05:44.750
You know, when the Human Genome Project was
invested in for sequencing, it was 3 percent

01:05:44.750 --> 01:05:47.330
bolted on for ethics discussions.

01:05:47.330 --> 01:05:51.440
The problem with funding things on that basis
is it's typically all piecemeal.

01:05:51.440 --> 01:05:55.170
If you have to do significant new regulatory
science, if you have to do something that

01:05:55.170 --> 01:05:57.829
requires block funding, you just can't get
the support for it.

01:05:57.829 --> 01:06:03.290
So I think there needs to be a holistic reconsideration
of how we're strategically approaching biology-period.

01:06:03.290 --> 01:06:04.930
NICHOLS: Thank you, Drew.

01:06:04.930 --> 01:06:09.610
The OSTP he mentioned, that's the Office of
Science and Technology Policy in the White

01:06:09.610 --> 01:06:10.610
House.

01:06:10.610 --> 01:06:14.760
And I think Drew is correct, at the White
House and at the director of NIH and the director

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:19.770
of CDC, those are the leaders that will put
together the policy pillars-

01:06:19.770 --> 01:06:20.950
GARRETT: For the United States.

01:06:20.950 --> 01:06:22.070
NICHOLS: Yeah, of course.

01:06:22.070 --> 01:06:26.050
GARRETT: And for publicly funded science.

01:06:26.050 --> 01:06:28.840
But I mean, let's keep in mind-
NICHOLS: But those norms usually apply.

01:06:28.840 --> 01:06:32.470
GARRETT: There's-90 percent, you know, out
there is not the United States doing this

01:06:32.470 --> 01:06:33.470
science.

01:06:33.470 --> 01:06:36.200
And a huge percent of it is in private sector.

01:06:36.200 --> 01:06:44.460
So you know, the only thing I would say to
add to what Drew-Drew's really smart analysis,

01:06:44.460 --> 01:06:50.810
is to-so that you don't all go out of here
paralyzed with terror and fear.

01:06:50.810 --> 01:06:55.890
There is a self-correcting going on all the
time within the scientific community.

01:06:55.890 --> 01:07:01.980
And just a great example, because yesterday
was worldwide Subway Microbiome Day.

01:07:01.980 --> 01:07:08.610
And so in 54 cities around the world, teenagers
and college students were out swabbing subways

01:07:08.610 --> 01:07:14.480
and bringing samples back to figure out what
microbes were in all the subways of the world.

01:07:14.480 --> 01:07:19.619
This is a build-on from a study done just
in the New York subway systems two years ago,

01:07:19.619 --> 01:07:27.290
that had a really bad outcome, which is to
say they went public with their findings,

01:07:27.290 --> 01:07:33.860
even though they didn't have great reference
strains to-reference sequences to be sure

01:07:33.860 --> 01:07:37.930
what strains they were really finding, and
whether it was accurate or not.

01:07:37.930 --> 01:07:43.100
And here in a city where we were subjected
to 9/11 and an anthrax attack, they said they

01:07:43.100 --> 01:07:49.550
found plague and anthrax in our subways, and
were very surprised that the public was upset

01:07:49.550 --> 01:07:53.730
and that the city health department was less
than happy with their announcement.

01:07:53.730 --> 01:07:55.560
A learning curve has happened.

01:07:55.560 --> 01:08:01.030
A lot of scientists piled onto those folks
and said, let's do this right.

01:08:01.030 --> 01:08:04.970
Let's be much more accurate, much more careful.

01:08:04.970 --> 01:08:07.600
Let's think about what we're saying to the
public.

01:08:07.600 --> 01:08:10.350
If this is a public good, let's frame it right.

01:08:10.350 --> 01:08:16.139
And now, yesterday, 54 cities executed-under
a whole kind of mutually agreed framework

01:08:16.139 --> 01:08:18.610
that's a really sophisticated improvement.

01:08:18.610 --> 01:08:20.299
NICHOLS: Thank you, Laurie.

01:08:20.299 --> 01:08:23.640
I think-join me in thanking our panel.

01:08:23.640 --> 01:08:24.640
(Applause.)

01:08:24.640 --> 01:08:26.259
GARRETT: Thank you, Drew.

01:08:26.259 --> 01:08:29.420
NICHOLS: Thank you, Drew, from Stanford.

01:08:29.420 --> 01:08:30.420
(Laughter.)

01:08:30.420 --> 01:08:30.469
(END)

